    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical aspect. The pitot tube measures the dynamic pressure of the air, which is the pressure exerted by the air flowing over a surface. This dynamic pressure is proportional to the square of the airspeed.\n\nThe pitot tube is typically connected to a static port, which measures the static pressure of the air. The static pressure is the pressure exerted by the air at a given altitude and temperature, and it's not directly affected by the airspeed.\n\nThe pressure difference between the pitot tube and the static port is proportional to the dynamic pressure, which is in turn proportional to the airspeed. By measuring this pressure difference, the pitot tube can provide an accurate measurement of the airspeed.\n\nTo understand this better, imagine a goblin trying to navigate a narrow tunnel. The airspeed is like the goblin's speed through the tunnel, and the pressure difference is like the resistance the goblin encounters as it moves through the tunnel. The more the goblin speeds up, the greater the resistance, and the greater the pressure difference. By measuring this pressure difference, the goblin (or in this case, the airspeed indicator) can determine its",
